CAVEAT:  This run did not establish the following.
         1. The restart was a `reload`. An abrupt power interruption is the harder test,
            and it was attempted first on this bench. It could not be achieved here, so
            it was not measured. The `reload` is still a real discriminator, for a
            reason this run measured and did not assume: the volatile log is a tmpfs,
            which is a filesystem in RAM, and a `reload` on this platform is a full
            kernel restart (STEP 3 names the media and STEP 5 shows the result).
         2. Only one filter level was used for the permanent log (informational on top
            of the default warnings). Program, facility, message-text and exclude
            filters were not measured.
         3. The permanent log's 50 kb size limit and its rotation behaviour under a full
            log were not tested. Rotation was only observed at boot.
         4. The run used one DUT in a standalone (not stacked) configuration.
