  The volatile side, from the same output:
    Buffered log:  enabled, 50kb, Filters *1 Level notices
    Console log:   enabled,        Filters *1 Level critical
    External log:  DISABLED

awplus# show running-config | include log
  aaa authentication login default local     <-- no `log` configuration of any kind
awplus# show startup-config | include log
  aaa authentication login default local

  The permanent log is already enabled, and its filter is `warnings`. That is one level
  more severe than the buffered log's `notices`. This difference is what STEP 1 uses.

  Note for the next person: there is no `show log buffered` command on this platform.
awplus# show log buffered | include CKT33402-NOTICE
  ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.
  The volatile log is read with plain `show log` and `show log tail N`. The permanent
  log is read with `show log permanent`.

STEP 1 -- control: a notice is not stored under the default filter              PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: with the default `warnings` filter in force, produce a notice-level event. It
  must appear in the volatile log and must not appear in the permanent log. This shows
  the permanent log is selective, so a later presence is a real result and not simply
  "the device keeps everything".

awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
awplus# show log | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:35:35 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:36:19 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:36:24 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
                                            <-- present in the volatile log, three times

awplus# show log permanent | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
                                            <-- no output: not in the permanent log

  The same absence, counted at the filesystem level rather than inferred:
[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-NOTICE-1 /flash/log/messages /flash/log/messages.1 /flash/log/messages.2
  /flash/log/messages:0
  /flash/log/messages.1:0
  /flash/log/messages.2:0

  => STEP 1 PASS: the permanent log is selective. A notice-level event is accepted by the
  volatile log and refused by the permanent log, because the permanent filter is
  `warnings`. Everything stored later in this case is stored because the filter was
  changed on purpose.

STEP 2 -- raise the filter and write three markers, one of them after `write`   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# log permanent level informational
awplus(config)# end
awplus# show running-config | include log
  log permanent level informational
  aaa authentication login default local

awplus# show log config
    Permanent log:
      Status ......... enabled
      Maximum size ... 50kb
      Filters:
     *1   Level ...... warnings      Program .... any   Facility ... any
          Msg text ... any           Type ....... include
      2   Level ...... informational Program .... any   Facility ... any
          Msg text ... any           Type ....... include
      Statistics ..... 1777 messages received, 43 accepted, 0 excluded

  The command did not change the existing filter. It added a second one. The default
  `*1 Level warnings` is still there. Both filters are `Type ... include`, so a message
  is stored if either filter accepts it, and informational and above now pass.

  The three markers:
  13:40:17  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-1      <-- before the write
  13:40:19  awplus# write
            Building configuration...
            [OK]
  13:40:20  awplus# show startup-config | include log
            log permanent level informational
            aaa authentication login default local
  13:40:21  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-2      <-- after the write
  13:41:34  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-3      <-- 1 m 42 s before restart

  Marker 2 and marker 3 were made after the configuration was saved. If they survive the
  restart, then what survived is the log itself being written to non-volatile storage as
  each event happens. It is not a side effect of the `write` that saved the
  configuration.

  Confirmed stored before anything was interrupted, so that a later absence could not be
  read two ways:
awplus# show log permanent | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:16 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:20 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-2
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:37 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show log permanent | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:41 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:41:33 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-3

  => STEP 2 PASS: with the filter raised, notice-level events are stored. Five entries
  were present in the permanent log before the restart: the three markers and the two
  read commands.

STEP 3 -- where the two logs are actually stored                                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: "non-volatile storage" is a claim about media, and no `show` command on this
  device names the media. It was read directly, in a `start-shell` root shell on the
  DUT. Only read commands were used in that shell. `start-shell` is logged by the device
  (`IMISH[...]: [manager@ttyS0]start-shell`), so the session can be audited.

[root@awplus ~]# mount | grep -E " /flash | /var/log "
  none on /var/log type tmpfs (rw,relatime,size=10240k)
  /dev/mmcblk0p1 on /flash type ext3 (rw,nosuid,nodev,relatime)

[root@awplus ~]# ls -l /flash/log/
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root          5957 Oct 22 00:41 messages
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root         10546 Oct 22 00:24 messages.1
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root         11578 Oct 21 23:30 messages.2
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root         11862 Oct 21 04:29 messages.3

[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-PERM /flash/log/*
  /flash/log/messages:4
  /flash/log/messages.1:0
  /flash/log/messages.2:0
  /flash/log/messages.3:0

  => STEP 3 PASS: the two logs are on two different kinds of media. The volatile log is
  /var/log/messages on a tmpfs of 10240k, which is a filesystem held in RAM. The
  permanent log is /flash/log/messages on /dev/mmcblk0p1, an ext3 filesystem on the eMMC
  device. The markers were already in the flash copy before the restart. The rotation
  times of messages.1, .2 and .3 match earlier restarts, so the permanent log rotates at
  each boot and keeps about a day of history.

STEP 4 -- interrupting the device                                          PARTIAL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method as designed: first an abrupt power interruption, which is the harder test,
  because the device then has no chance to write anything out. Fallback: `reload`.

  The abrupt power interruption was attempted first, at 13:41:36 NZDT. It could not be
  achieved on this bench. The DUT did not lose power and did not restart:

awplus# show system | include Uptime
  Uptime             : 0 days 00:18:31
  Read at 13:42:05 NZDT. 13:42:05 minus 18 m 31 s is 13:23:34, which is the previous
  restart of this unit, before this case started. So no restart happened.
  `show reboot history` gained no entry either, and the health sample after the case
  shows only one new reboot entry, which is the `reload` below.

  The device kept running through the attempt and its environment reads stayed normal.
  The abrupt interruption was therefore not measured, and this log does not claim it was.

  The fallback was used:
  box: tools/tb ckreload tb277 oper1022 u2 115200 600
    13:43:18 >>> reload
    reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
    13:44:18 login seen after 60 s
  (exit 0)

  A login prompt is not readiness, so the leg was polled until it answered:
    poll 1  4 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss
    poll 2  4 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss
    poll 3  4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss    <- 13:44:30 NZDT

  => STEP 4 PARTIAL: the device was restarted, but by `reload`, not by an abrupt power
  interruption. The abrupt interruption was attempted and could not be achieved on this
  bench, so that half of the step was not measured. The `reload` still discriminates
  between the two logs, for a reason STEP 3 measured: the volatile log lives on a tmpfs,
  which is RAM, and a `reload` on this platform is a full kernel restart. The boot's own
  kernel lines are in the health sample. RAM does not survive that.

