awplus# show running-config | include log   ->  log permanent level informational
                                                aaa authentication login default local
awplus# show startup-config | include log   ->  identical

  The same split at the filesystem level:
[root@awplus ~]# mount | grep -E " /flash | /var/log "
  none on /var/log type tmpfs (rw,relatime,size=10240k)
  /dev/mmcblk0p1 on /flash type ext3 (rw,nosuid,nodev,relatime)
[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-PERM /flash/log/messages /flash/log/messages.1 /flash/log/messages.2
  /flash/log/messages:0
  /flash/log/messages.1:2
  /flash/log/messages.2:5
[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-PERM /var/log/messages
  0

  The five entries from before the restart are in /flash/log/messages.2. That is the file
  which was the live permanent log before the restart and was rotated by it.

  One result was checked instead of assumed:
[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-PERM /var/log/messages.1
  2
  Two matches in a file on the tmpfs could look like content surviving in RAM. It is not:
[root@awplus ~]# head -1 /var/log/messages.1
  #3 2026-10-22T00:43:50+00:00 kern.notice awplus kernel: Linux version 6.12.110 ...
[root@awplus ~]# tail -1 /var/log/messages.1
  #1487 2026-10-22T00:44:59+00:00 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]terminal no monitor
  The file starts at this boot's own kernel line, at 00:43:50. Its two matches are the
  two reads issued at 00:44:47 and 00:44:51, after the restart.

  => STEP 5 PASS: the numbers close exactly. 5 occurrences from before the restart, all
  of them only on flash and none of them in RAM. 2 occurrences from after the restart, in
  both places. So entries written before a restart are readable afterwards from the
  permanent log with nothing typed, the same entries are gone from the volatile log, and
  the filter's earlier decision survived as well.

