STEP 7 -- the restored filter re-proved on a fresh event                        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: do not take the removal from the absence of an error. Produce one more
  notice-level event and show it is not stored.

awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE
awplus# show log | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE
  2026 Oct 22 00:47:18 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE
  2026 Oct 22 00:47:23 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE

[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE /var/log/messages /var/log/messages.1
  /var/log/messages:3
  /var/log/messages.1:0
[root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE /flash/log/messages /flash/log/messages.1
  /flash/log/messages:0
  /flash/log/messages.1:0

  => STEP 7 PASS: 3 occurrences in the volatile log and 0 on flash. With the filter back
  at `warnings`, a notice-level event is again not stored. The device is in the state it
  started in, and that is shown, not assumed.

  Method note for the next person: `show log permanent | include <pattern>` does not
  return a prompt when the pattern matches nothing. It prints no output and a console
  driver then waits out its whole timeout. Prove an absence with
  `grep -c <pattern> /flash/log/messages*` in a `start-shell`, which returns a definite 0.
  That is how the absences in STEP 1 and STEP 7 are proved here.
